 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Water-borne diseases are caused by germs (bacteria, viruses, parasites) carried in dirty water, or in food washed or prepared with it.\nHow the water gets dirty: human and animal faeces near streams and wells, open defecation, dirty containers, floods.\nCommon water-borne diseases:\n• cholera: severe watery diarrhoea and vomiting;\n• typhoid fever: long high fever, headache, stomach pain;\n• dysentery: diarrhoea with blood;\n• diarrhoeal diseases in children, the main danger being loss of water;\n• hepatitis A: jaundice (yellow eyes and skin);\n• bilharzia: caught by bathing or wading in infected streams; blood in the urine.\nTreatment: see a health worker; for diarrhoea, give oral rehydration solution (ORS) to replace lost water and salts.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "c": "a stream near the village latrines"
  },
  "problem": "Families drink untreated water from {c}. Which diseases might they catch, and why?",
  "steps": [
   "Germs from faeces in the latrines can get into the stream.",
   "Drinking the water can cause cholera, typhoid, dysentery or hepatitis A; bathing in it can spread bilharzia."
  ],
  "answer": "Cholera, typhoid, dysentery, hepatitis A (and bilharzia), from germs in faeces.",
  "checks": []
 },
